<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Fun Projects on Hadobot Blog</title><link>https://hadobot.github.io/tags/fun-projects/</link><description>Recent content in Fun Projects on Hadobot Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 02 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://hadobot.github.io/tags/fun-projects/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Do You Know Code? Have Some Fun with Morse Code</title><link>https://hadobot.github.io/p/do-you-know-code-have-some-fun-with-morse-code/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://hadobot.github.io/p/do-you-know-code-have-some-fun-with-morse-code/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="introduction-to-morse-code-communication"&gt;Introduction to Morse Code Communication
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The art of communication has undergone a significant transformation over the years. Before the advent of the internet, people relied on various methods to send messages across long distances. One such iconic system that played a crucial role in shaping the course of communication history is &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;Morse code&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. This simple yet effective method of communication used a series of dots and dashes to transmit messages across telegraphs and radio waves, revolutionizing the way people connected with each other.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Morse code has a rich history, dating back to the early days of telegraphy. Developed by Samuel Morse and his colleagues in the 1830s, it was widely used for decades to transmit messages over wires and radio waves. The code consists of a series of dots (short signals) and dashes (long signals) that correspond to letters, numbers, and other characters. This unique system allowed people to communicate over long distances, facilitating global communication and playing a vital role in shaping modern communication systems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this article, we&amp;rsquo;ll take a closer look at the basics of Morse code, its history, and its significance in the evolution of communication technology. We&amp;rsquo;ll also explore a simple Arduino project that brings this vintage technology to life using just the built-in LED. By blinking messages in Morse code, we&amp;rsquo;ll demonstrate how this ancient communication method can be reimagined in a modern context, providing a fun and educational experience for electronics enthusiasts and communication buffs alike.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="project-overview"&gt;Project Overview
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;This project leverages Arduino&amp;rsquo;s built-in LED to transmit Morse code messages. Here&amp;rsquo;s how it works:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each letter of your message is translated into a unique pattern of dots and dashes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;These patterns are then transmitted using the LED on your Arduino board, with short blinks representing dots and long blinks representing dashes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;By changing the message string in the code and uploading it to your Arduino, you can send any message you like in Morse code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id="project-steps"&gt;Project Steps
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Modify the message string in the code to your desired text.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upload the code to your Arduino board.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Watch as your board transmits your message in Morse code using the built-in LED.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
